{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["macOS"],"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"FixedStar"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"mentions":["doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/CelestialPositions"],"kind":"mentions"},{"content":[{"anchor":"overview","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Fixed stars are celestial objects whose positions are essentially constant","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"on the celestial sphere. This struct stores catalog coordinates and provides"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"position calculations at any time."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Unlike solar system bodies (which require orbital calculations), fixed stars","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"are defined by their J2000 mean equator coordinates and distance. The library"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"handles precession, nutation, and aberration automatically.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Example","anchor":"Example","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["let algol = FixedStar(","    name: \"Algol\",","    rightAscension: 3.136148,  \/\/ J2000 RA in hours","    declination: 40.9556,      \/\/ J2000 Dec in degrees","    distance: 92.95    \/\/ Light-years",")","","let longitude = try algol.eclipticLongitude(at: .now)","print(\"Algol is at \\(longitude)°\")"],"type":"codeListing"},{"anchor":"Coordinate-Sources","type":"heading","text":"Coordinate Sources","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"J2000 coordinates can be found from:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"https:\/\/simbad.cds.unistra.fr\/simbad\/","isActive":true}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Hipparcos Catalog","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Yale Bright Star Catalog","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Concurrency","anchor":"Concurrency"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The C library exposes eight user-defined star slots ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"BODY_STAR1"},{"type":"text","text":"…"},{"code":"BODY_STAR8","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"),"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each an independent record. Calculations map a star to a slot by a stable hash of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"its coordinates, so up to eight distinct stars can be computed fully in parallel."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"A slot is only redefined when the star currently loaded in it differs, so repeated"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"calls for the same star skip the redefinition entirely. Stars whose hashes collide"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"on the same slot serialize on that slot’s mutex (correct, just slower)."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"kind":"symbol","sections":[],"seeAlsoSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/AdvancedFeatures","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/Chiron","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/GravitySimulation","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/LagrangePoint","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/LagrangePointID","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/Jupiter","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/JupiterMoons","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/RotationAxis","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/Atmosphere"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Advanced","title":"Advanced"}],"abstract":[{"text":"A fixed star defined by its J2000 equatorial coordinates.","type":"text"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Ast